  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$\\int 4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
